1. e4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e5 3. Bc4 Nc6 4. Nf3 Bc5 5. Nd5 {This unusual move prepares a pawn sacrifice that turns pianissimo into fortissimo!} d6 6. d4 $6 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 Nxd5 8. Bb5+ c6 ({Preferring complications to the defensive} 8... Kf8 9. Nb3 $1) 9. Nxc6 $1 Qh4 $2 ({Now Black should lose.} 9... Qb6 {looks a good fighting chance which does not expose his queen.}) 10. Nxe5+ Ke7 11. O-O dxe5 12. Qxd5 Bd4 13. b3 ({He can also win by either} 13. c3 {or 13 Be3, but tthis is the quick and brilliant way.}) 13... Bxa1 14. Ba3+ Kf6 15. Qd6+ Kg5 16. Bc1+ $2 ({The wrong sequence of checks. He should bring his queen into the attack by} 16. Qe7+ {and after} f6 (16... Kh5 17. Qxf7+ g6 18. Be2+ Bg4 19. f3 Bd4+ 20. Kh1 Bc8 21. g4+ $18) 17. Qxg7+ Kh5 18. Be2+ Bg4 19. Qf7+ {White wins quickly}) 16... Kh5 17. Be2+ Bg4 18. f3 Be6 $1 19. f4+ Bg4 20. fxe5 $2 ({Stockfish 17:} 20. Qd7 $18) 20... Bxe2 21. Qd7 Bxf1 22. Qf5+ g5 23. Qxf7+ Kh6 24. Qf6+ {Source: Birmingham Mail, 24 April 1971 (n.b. date is approximate)} 1/2-1/2

