1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 Ke8 10. Ne2 Be6 11. Nf4 Bd5 12. Nxd5 cxd5 13. Re1 $6 (13. g4 $14 {Miles}) 13... Bc5 14. Bf4 c6 15. Rad1 h6 16. h3 $6 (16. g4) 16... g5 $1 17. Bc1 Ke7 18. Nh2 $6 Rag8 19. Ng4 h5 20. Ne3 $17 {Miles offered a draw. "If he had played better up until then I would have taken the draw." (Bisguier in Chess Life & Review)} Nh4 $2 21. Nxd5+ $3 cxd5 22. Rxd5 b6 $2 23. e6 f5 $2 24. Rd7+ Kf6 $2 25. Bd2 Kg6 26. b4 Bf8 27. e7 Bg7 28. Re6+ Kh7 29. Bxg5 Ng6 30. Rxa7 Re8 31. Rd7 Ne5 32. Rd5 Rhg8 33. f4 Nc4 34. Rxf5 Bd4+ 35. Kh1 Ne3 36. Rf7+ Rg7 37. Rh6+ {Sources: CHESS/38/673-674 May 1973, p237; Chess Life & Review, July 1973, p381; BCM, June 1973, p228; The Times, 5 May 1973; Sunday Times, 10 June 1973; "It's Only Me" Ed. Geoff Lawton, Batsford, 2003, p26} (37. Rh6+ Kg8 38. Rf8+ Rxf8 39. Rh8+ Kxh8 40. exf8=Q+ {winning easily. I'm an incurable optimist I know but I think as Black I would have played these moves - just in case, after} Kh7 {White in the general excitement played} 41. Qd6 $2 {after which} Nf5 $1 {and White cannot avoid perpetual check (C Hugh O'D Alexander)}) 1-0

